      "AbstractText": "Menstrual parameters and gynecologic symptoms of 389 women who underwent laparoscopic tuabl banding were studied prospectively and longitudinally. Previous oral contraceptive (OC) users exhibited an immediate increase in menstrual flow and dysmenorrhea, which declined slightly with time. No such changes occurred in women who had used other methods of temporary contraception. A significant increase in noncyclic pelvin pain, independent of previous contraceptive usage, developed only in women 38 years of age. Menstrual cycles and dyspareunia were not affected. A small control group of women whose husbands underwent vasectomy showed no significant changes in any of these parameters."
